://www.w3.org/about/diversity/)\n\n Corporation (https://www.w3.org/about/corporation/)\n\n Policies & legal information (https://www.w3.org/policies/)\n\n Contact us (https://www.w3.org/contact/)\n\n Help (https://www.w3.org/help/)\n\n Search (https://www.w3.org/help/search/)\n\n My account (https://www.w3.org/users/myprofile/)\n\n Home (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n W3C standards and drafts (https://www.w3.org/TR/)\n\n W3C standards and drafts\n\n W3C publishes a range of technical reports (Standards and supporting Notes) which help move the web forward.\n\n Search for terms such as API, Internationalization, or WAI-ARIA\n\n Not sure where to start? Search for Standards only (https://www.w3.org/TR/?status%5B0%5D=standard),\n any\n reports in progress (https://www.w3.org/TR/?status%5B0%5D=draftStandard&status%5B1%5D=candidateStandard&status%5B2%5D=note&status%5B3%5D=dry&status%5B4%5D=cry), everything tagged\n with Accessibility (https://www.w3.org/TR/?tags%5B0%5D=accessibility), or all reports (https://www.w3.org/TR/).\n\n Advanced filter options \n \n Filter by status\n\n Standards \n \n W3C publishes Recommendations which are considered Web Standards. Standards are normative. They are endorsed by W3C and its Members. Learn about our Standards process .\n\n Draft Standards \n\n Candidate Standards \n\n Standards \n\n Supporting Notes \n \n Notes are informative. They give you use cases, practices, and code examples.\n\n Draft Notes \n\n Notes \n\n Statements \n\n Supporting Registries \n \n Registries are informative. They document collections of values or other data.\n\n Draft Registries \n\n Candidate Registries \n\n Registries \n\n Filter by tags\n\n Accessibility \n\n Browser \n\n CSS \n\n Data \n\n DOM \n\n Digital Publishing \n\n Graphics \n\n HTML \n\n HTTP \n\n i18n \n\n Media \n\n Performance \n\n Privacy \n\n Protocol \n\n Security \n\n Web API \n\n Web Fonts \n\n WoT \n\n XML \n\n Show results \n Clear all (https://www.w3.org/TR/)\n\n Showing 1239 \n reports