ur mission (https://www.w3.org/mission/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Leadership (https://www.w3.org/about/leadership/)\n\n Staff (https://www.w3.org/staff/)\n\n Careers (https://www.w3.org/careers/)\n\n Media kit (https://www.w3.org/about/press-media/)\n\n Diversity (https://www.w3.org/about/diversity/)\n\n Corporation (https://www.w3.org/about/corporation/)\n\n Policies & legal information (https://www.w3.org/policies/)\n\n Contact us (https://www.w3.org/contact/)\n\n Help (https://www.w3.org/help/)\n\n Search (https://www.w3.org/help/search/)\n\n My account (https://www.w3.org/users/myprofile/)\n\n W3C Authentication Platform\n\n Please log in using your W3C Account.\n If you have registered one or several second factors, you\'ll be prompted for them as a second step.\n\n Don\'t have an account? Create one (https://www.w3.org/account/request/).\n\n Username (Required) \n\n Password (Required) \n\n Forgot password? (https://www.w3.org/account/recover/)\n\n Remember me on this computer \n\n Login \n\n Home (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n Contact (https://www.w3.org/contact/)\n\n Help (https://www.w3.org/help/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Legal & Policies (https://www.w3.org/policies/)\n\n Corporation (https://www.w3.org/about/corporation/)\n\n System Status (https://status.w3.org/)\n\n W3C on Mastodon (https://w3c.social/@w3c)\n\n W3C on GitHub (https://github.com/w3c/)\n\n Copyright &copy; 2026 World Wide Web Consortium (https://www.w3.org/).\n W3C &reg; liability , trademark and permissive license (https://www.w3.org/copyright/document-license/) rules apply.','d8d95b0ed3f8fd1e60a7fa8316ff5059d8759c36e50c8b4b5767f72ea576164a',NULL,NULL,'2026-10-05 07:46:09','INDEXED','2026-10-05 07:41:28','2026-10-05 07:46:09'),(875,7,36,'218bd0da026f0167501452cf7294ad24958b9c5a103b47f9bd3d53e753ffae6e','https://www.w3.org/support-us/','Support us | W3C','Support us | W3C \n\n Skip to content \n\n 日本語ホームページJapanese website (https://www.w3.org/ja/)\n\n 简体中文首页Chinese website (https://www.w3.org/zh-hans/)\n\n Visit the W3C homepage (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n Standards & groups (https://www.w3.org/standards/)\n\n Standards & groups\n\n Understand the various specifications, their maturity levels on the web standards track, their adoption, and the groups that develop them.\n\n Explore web standards (https://www.w3.org/standards/)\n\n About W3C web standards (https://www.w3.org/standards/about/)\n\n W3C standards & drafts (https://www.w3.org/TR/)\n\n Types of documents W3C publishes (https://www.w3.org/standards/types/)\n\n W3C groups (https://www.w3.org/groups/)\n\n Translations of W3C standards & drafts (https://www.w3.org/translations/)\n\n Reviews & public feedback (https://www.w3.org/standards/review/)\n\n Promote web standards (https://www.w3.org/get-involved/promote/)\n\n Liaisons (https://www.w3.org/liaisons/)\n\n Technical Architecture Group (TAG) (https://www.w3.org/groups/other/tag/)\n\n Get involved (https://www.w3.org/get-involved/)\n\n Get involved\n\n W3C works at the nexus of core technology, industry needs, and societal needs.\n\n Find ways to get involved (https://www.w3.org/get-involved/)\n\n Browse our work by industry (https://www.w3.org/ecosystems/)\n\n Become a Member (https://www.w3.org/membership/)\n\n Member home (restricted) (https://www.w3.org/Member/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Mailing lists (https://www.w3.org/email/)\n\n Participant guidebook (https://www.w3.org/guide/)\n\n Positive work environment (https://www.w3.org/about/positive-work-environment/)\n\n Invited Experts (https://www.w3.org/invited-experts/)\n\n Resources (https://www.w3.org/resources/)\n\n Resources\n\n Master web fundamentals, use our developer tools, or contribute code.\n\n Learn from W3C resources (https://www.w3.org/resources/)\n\n Developers (https://www.w3.org/developers/)\n\n Validators & tools (https://www.w3.org/developers/tools/)\n\n Accessibility fundamentals (https://www.w3.org/WAI/fundamentals/)\n\n Internationalization (i18n) (https://www.w3.org/International/i18n-drafts/nav/about)\n\n Translations of W3C standards & drafts (https://www.w3.org/translations/)\n\n Code of conduct (https://www.w3.org/policies/code-of-conduct/)\n\n Reports (https://www.w3.org/reports/)\n\n News & events (https://www.w3.org/news-events/)\n\n News & events\n\n Recent content across news, blogs, press releases, media; upcoming events.\n\n Explore news & events (https://www.w3.org/news-events/)\n\n News (https://www.w3.org/news/)\n\n Blog (https://www.w3.org/blog/)\n\n Press releases (https://www.w3.org/press-releases/)\n\n Press & media (https://www.w3.org/about/press-media/)\n\n Events (https://www.w3.org/events/)\n\n Annual W3C Conference (TPAC) (https://www.w3.org/news-events/tpac/)\n\n Code of conduct (https://www.w3.org/policies/code-of-conduct/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Support us\n\n Make a huge difference to our operations as a public-interest non-profit, and help us to achieve our vision.\n\n Ways you can support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n About (https://www.w3.org/about/)\n\n About\n\n Understand our values and principles, learn our history, look into our policies, meet our people.\n\n Find out more about us (https://www.w3.org/about/)\n\n Our mission (https://www.w3.org/mission/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Leadership (https://www.w3.org/about/leadership/)\n\n Staff (https://www.w3.org/staff/)\n\n Careers (https://www.w3.org/careers/)\n\n Media kit (https://www.w3.org/about/press-media/)\n\n Diversity (https://www.w3.org/about/diversity/)\n\n Corporation (https://www.w3.org/about/corporation/)\n\n Policies & legal information (https://www.w3.org/policies/)\n\n Contact us (https://www.w3.org/contact/)\n\n Help (https://www.w3.org/help/)\n\n Search (https://www.w3.org/help/search/)\n\n My account (https://www.w3.org/users/myprofile/)\n\n Home (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Support us\n\n Read this page in: \n\n 中文（简体） (https://www.w3.org/zh-hans/support-us/)\n\n 日本語 (https://www.w3.org/ja/support-us/)\n\n The support from generous individuals and organizations such as you, who share our values, make a huge difference to our operations as a public-interest non-profit, and help us to achieve our vision.\n\n Why support W3C? We work to support the community that develops web standards, the building blocks of our digitally connected world. We work to ensure the long-term growth of the open, interoperable web that caters for accessibility, privacy, security, and internationalization.\n\n Ways you can support the World Wide Web Consortium\n\n Donations\n\n Support our vision for a web that works, for everyone. W3C recognizes that giving takes many forms. Make a one-time or regular donation, or reach out to discuss how our work aligns with your giving portfolio.\n\n Donate (https://www.w3.org/support-us/donate/)\n\n Sponsoring\n\n Your sponsorship can directly contribute to our work on accessibility (https://www.w3.org/mission/accessibility/), internationalization (https://www.w3.org/mission/internationalization/), privacy (https://www.w3.org/mission/privacy/) and security (https://www.w3.org/mission/security/) which guide our technical work and community engagement, and support business ecosystem development.\n\n Sponsor (https://www.w3.org/support-us/sponsor/)\n\n Partnering\n\n We are actively seeking funding opportunities to expand our work and support our community. Get in touch if you are interested in partnering with us through grants, fellowship, and other mechanisms, to strengthen our programming.\n\n Contact us\n\nWorld Wide Web Consortium, Inc. is a nonprofit, tax-exempt charitable organization under Section 501(c)(3) of the U.S. Internal Revenue Code EIN: 84-4023862. \r\n\n Related pages\n\n About us (https://www.w3.org/about/)\n\n This page introduces the World Wide Web Consortium, a multi-stakeholder member-organization which develops web standards in the open.\n\n Page\n\n Our mission (https://www.w3.org/mission/)\n\n Learn who W3C is, our values and the principles upon which we conduct our mission to make the web work — for everyone.\n\n Page\n\n Resources (https://www.w3.org/resources/)\n\n This page lists the various resources to help W3C Members, web developers or the public, such as mailing lists, blogs and microblogs, news, training, tutorials, tools, videos, translations, etc.\n\n Page\n\n Fundraising policy (https://www.w3.org/policies/fundraising-policy/)\n\n This policy outlines the principles and conditions around the solicitation, acceptance, and management of in-kind or financial contributions to W3C activities that are outside of member-dues.\n\n Page\n\n Home (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n Contact (https://www.w3.org/contact/)\n\n Help (https://www.w3.org/help/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Legal & Policies (https://www.w3.org/policies/)\n\n Corporation (https://www.w3.org/about/corporation/)\n\n System Status (https://status.w3.org/)\n\n W3C on Mastodon (https://w3c.social/@w3c)\n\n W3C on GitHub (https://github.com/w3c/)\n\n Copyright &copy; 2026 World Wide Web Consortium (https://www.w3.org/).\n W3C &reg; liability , trademark and permissive license (https://www.w3.org/copyright/document-license/) rules apply.','e9f0a3d81a32eff4625228a8f84db3db1f7828269f59f9f25d4b425c60542e0a',NULL,NULL,'2026-10-05 07:46:09','INDEXED','2026-10-05 07:41:28','2026-10-05 07:46:09'),(876,7,36,'218bd0da026f0167501452cf7294ad24958b9c5a103b47f9bd3d53e753ffae6e','https://www.w3.org/email/','Mailing lists | W3C','Mailing lists | W3C \n\n Skip to content \n\n 日本語ホームページJapanese website (https://www.w3.org/ja/)\n\n 简体中文首页Chinese website (https://www.w3.org/zh-hans/)\n\n Visit the W3C homepage (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n Standards & groups (https://www.w3.org/standards/)\n\n Standards & groups\n\n Understand the various specifications, their maturity levels on the web standards track, their adoption, and the groups that develop them.\n\n Explore web standards (https://www.w3.org/standards/)\n\n About W3C web standards (https://www.w3.org/standards/about/)\n\n W3C standards & drafts (https://www.w3.org/TR/)\n\n Types of documents W3C publishes (https://www.w3.org/standards/types/)\n\n W3C groups (https://www.w3.org/groups/)\n\n Translations of W3C standards & drafts (https://www.w3.org/translations/)\n\n Reviews & public feedback (https://www.w3.org/standards/review/)\n\n Promote web standards (https://www.w3.org/get-involved/promote/)\n\n Liaisons (https://www.w3.org/liaisons/)\n\n Technical Architecture Group (TAG) (https://www.w3.org/groups/other/tag/)\n\n Get involved (https://www.w3.org/get-involved/)\n\n Get involved\n\n W3C works at the nexus of core technology, industry needs, and societal needs.\n\n Find ways to get involved (https://www.w3.org/get-involved/)\n\n Browse our work by industry (https://www.w3.org/ecosystems/)\n\n Become a Member (https://www.w3.org/membership/)\n\n Member home (restricted) (https://www.w3.org/Member/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Mailing lists (https://www.w3.org/email/)\n\n Participant guidebook (https://www.w3.org/guide/)\n\n Positive work environment (https://www.w3.org/about/positive-work-environment/)\n\n Invited Experts (https://www.w3.org/invited-experts/)\n\n Resources (https://www.w3.org/resources/)\n\n Resources\n\n Master web fundamentals, use our developer tools, or contribute code.\n\n Learn from W3C resources (https://www.w3.org/resources/)\n\n Developers (https://www.w3.org/developers/)\n\n Validators & tools (https://www.w3.org/developers/tools/)\n\n Accessibility fundamentals (https://www.w3.org/WAI/fundamentals/)\n\n Internationalization (i18n) (https://www.w3.org/International/i18n-drafts/nav/about)\n\n Translations of W3C standards & drafts (https://www.w3.org/translations/)\n\n Code of conduct (https://www.w3.org/policies/code-of-conduct/)\n\n Reports (https://www.w3.org/reports/)\n\n News & events (https://www.w3.org/news-events/)\n\n News & events\n\n Recent content across news, blogs, press releases, media; upcoming events.\n\n Explore news & events (https://www.w3.org/news-events/)\n\n News (https://www.w3.org/news/)\n\n Blog (https://www.w3.org/blog/)\n\n Press releases (https://www.w3.org/press-releases/)\n\n Press & media (https://www.w3.org/about/press-media/)\n\n Events (https://www.w3.org/events/)\n\n Annual W3C Conference (TPAC) (https://www.w3.org/news-events/tpac/)\n\n Code of conduct (https://www.w3.org/policies/code-of-conduct/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Support us\n\n Make a huge difference to our operations as a public-interest non-profit, and help us to achieve our vision.\n\n Ways you can support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n About (https://www.w3.org/about/)\n\n About\n\n Understand our values and principles, learn our history, look into our policies, meet our people.\n\n Find out more about us (https://www.w3.org/about/)\n\n Our mission (https://www.w3.org/mission/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Leadership (https://www.w3.org/about/leadership/)\n\n Staff (https://www.w3.org/staff/)\n\n Careers (https://www.w3.org/careers/)\n\n Media kit (https://www.w3.org/about/press-media/)\n\n Diversity (https://www.w3.org/about/diversity/)\n\n Corporation (https://www.w3.org/about/corporation/)\n\n Policies & legal information (https://www.w3.org/policies/)\n\n Contact us (https://www.w3.org/contact/)\n\n Help (https://www.w3.org/help/)\n\n Search (https://www.w3.org/help/search/)\n\n My account (https://www.w3.org/users/myprofile/)\n\n Home (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n Mailing lists (https://www.w3.org/email/)\n\n Mailing lists\n\n Read this page in: \n\n 中文（简体） (https://www.w3.org/zh-hans/email/)\n\n 日本語 (https://www.w3.org/ja/email/)\n\n Page contents\n\n General information \n\n Subscribing and unsubscribing \n\n Searching and referencing \n\n Guidelines and policies \n\n W3C hosts thousands of mailing lists for our group members and the general public to discuss and send feedback on Web standards work at W3C. The corresponding mailing list archives (https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/), most of which allow anyone to join and participate, contain millions of messages dating back to the earliest days of the Web (https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/www-talk/1991SepOct/).\n\n Our Mailing list policies (https://www.w3.org/policies/email/) govern W3C email and mailing lists usage.\n\n General information\n\n Each W3C mailing list has its own policies regarding who may post to the list. Those subscribed to a list are generally able to post directly to the list without delay; those who are not may be subject to manual moderation.\n\n First-time posters to our lists with public archives (https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/) will need to give us permission to publish their message in our public archives using our archive approval system .\n\n Each mailing list has a specific purpose. Please avoid cross-posting to multiple lists.\n\n Posting of Unsolicited Bulk E-mail (UBE), or spam is strictly forbidden .\n\n Conference announcements and calls for papers or participation are generally not welcome on W3C lists. Please see our spam policy for more details.\n\n Messages in our mailing list archives are generally not removed or edited, except in very rare circumstances. See our Archive Editing Policy for more information.\n\n Subscribing and unsubscribing\n\n Members of W3C groups (https://www.w3.org/groups/) are automatically subscribed to the relevant list(s) when joining the group(s), and unsubscribed when leaving.\n\n Many of our public mailing lists (https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/) also allow subscriptions without being a member of a group, using an email command interface described below.\n\n W3C account holders can view a list of their current mailing list subscriptions (https://lists.w3.org/admin/subscriptions).\n\n Before joining a group or subscribing to a mailing list please be sure you have read our mailing list policies (https://www.w3.org/policies/email/) and agree to abide by our Code of Conduct (Code) (https://www.w3.org/policies/code-of-conduct/).\n\n Group-related mailing lists\n\n To subscribe or unsubscribe to mailing lists associated with a W3C group (https://www.w3.org/groups/), please visit the home page for the group(s) in question.\n\n A list of your current groups is available on your user profile page (https://www.w3.org/users/myprofile).\n\n Subscribing manually\n\n To subscribe to a W3C mailing list, for example www-talk , send \"subscribe\" in the subject of a message to the list name followed by -request at our domain, for example:\n\n To: www-talk-request@w3.org\n Subject: subscribe\n You should receive an automated response with instructions on how to confirm your subscription request. Note that you will not get an acknowledgement if you are already subscribed.\n\n Unsubscribing manually\n\n To unsubscribe from a mailing list, for example www-talk , send \"unsubscribe\" in the Subject of a message to the list name followed by \"-request\" at our domain, for example www-talk-request@w3.org :\n\n To: www-talk-request@w3.org\n Subject: unsubscribe\n To unsubscribe an email address other than your current address, specify the old address following unsubscribe in the Subject, for example:\n\n To: www-talk-request@w3.org\n Subject: unsubscribe old.address@example.org\n In either case you should receive an autoreply with instructions on how to confirm your request.\n\n Asking for help\n\n All mailing list administrative requests, including asking for help, must be sent to a *-request address (per RFC2142 (http://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc2142.txt)), never to the list itself. For a mailing list named list-name@w3.org , the associated request address would be list-name-request@w3.org .\n\n Searching and referencing\n\n An archive search tool (https://www.w3.org/Search/Mail) is available.\n\n It is frequently useful to refer to a message (e.g., from the body of an email or from a web page) using a URI to the message in the archive. In addition, you can use the message\'s own \"message-id\" and map it to the archive URI using the message-id mapping tool (https://www.w3.org/mid/).\n\n Guidelines and policies\n\n Mailing list policies (https://www.w3.org/policies/email/)\n\nPolicies that apply to our mailing lists, and answers to frequently asked questions.\n\n Spam policy \n\nHow we define spam, what we do about it, and why.\n\n W3C Positive Work Environment (https://www.w3.org/about/positive-work-environment/) and Code of Conduct (Code) (https://www.w3.org/policies/code-of-conduct/)\n\nW3C\'s policies for appropriate behavior, including on our mailing lists.\n\n Email forgery prevention using SPF (Sender Policy Framework) \n\nInformation on our use of SPF to block forged email and prevent email forgeries in our name.\n\n Guidelines for email attachment formats \n\nGuidelines on email attachments for messages sent to W3C mailing lists.\n\n Filtering email from W3C lists (https://www.w3.org/2006/tools/wiki/EmailClientForMailingListFiltering)\n\nSome advice on how to filter mail from W3C mailing lists in various email clients.\n\n Policy on subject tagging \n\nRationale for not adding tags to the subjects of our lists.\n\n Archive editing policy \n\nW3C policy on editing/removing posts in online mailing list archives.\n\n Email address obfuscation \n\nSome notes on the amount of email address obfuscation performed on messages in W3C\'s mailing list archives.\n\n Home (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n Contact (https://www.w3.org/contact/)\n\n Help (https://www.w3.org/help/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Legal & Policies (https://www.w3.org/policies/)\n\n Corporation (https://www.w3.org/about/corporation/)\n\n System Status (https://status.w3.org/)\n\n W3C on Mastodon (https://w3c.social/@w3c)\n\n W3C on GitHub (https://github.com/w3c/)\n\n Copyright &copy; 2026 World Wide Web Consortium (https://www.w3.org/).\n W3C &reg; liability , trademark and permissive license (https://www.w3.org/copyright/document-license/) rules apply.','c74e556b2891ab763a1ad01db47942a9df954ce100429afd72d606715cfe2668',NULL,NULL,'2026-10-05 07:46:09','INDEXED','2026-10-05 07:41:28','2026-10-05 07:46:09'),(877,7,36,'218bd0da026f0167501452cf7294ad24958b9c5a103b47f9bd3d53e753ffae6e','https://www.w3.org/guide/','The Art of Consensus (Guidebook)','The Art of Consensus (Guidebook) \n\n Skip to content \n\n 日本語ホームページJapanese website (https://www.w3.org/ja/)\n\n 简体中文首页Chinese website (https://www.w3.org/zh-hans/)\n\n Visit the W3C homepage (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n Standards & groups (https://www.w3.org/standards/)\n\n Standards & groups\n\n Understand the various specifications, their maturity levels on the web standards track, their adoption, and the groups that develop them.\n\n Explore web standards (https://www.w3.org/standards/)\n\n About W3C web standards (https://www.w3.org/standards/about/)\n\n W3C standards & drafts (https://www.w3.org/TR/)\n\n Types of documents W3C publishes (https://www.w3.org/standards/types/)\n\n W3C groups (https://www.w3.org/groups/)\n\n Translations of W3C standards & drafts (https://www.w3.org/translations/)\n\n Reviews & public feedback (https://www.w3.org/standards/review/)\n\n Promote web standards (https://www.w3.org/get-involved/promote/)\n\n Liaisons (https://www.w3.org/liaisons/)\n\n Technical Architecture Group (TAG) (https://www.w3.org/groups/other/tag/)\n\n Get involved (https://www.w3.org/get-involved/)\n\n Get involved\n\n W3C works at the nexus of core technology, industry needs, and societal needs.\n\n Find ways to get involved (https://www.w3.org/get-involved/)\n\n Browse our work by industry (https://www.w3.org/ecosystems/)\n\n Become a Member (https://www.w3.org/membership/)\n\n Member home (restricted) (https://www.w3.org/Member/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Mailing lists (https://www.w3.org/email/)\n\n Participant guidebook (https://www.w3.org/guide/)\n\n Positive work environment (https://www.w3.org/about/positive-work-environment/)\n\n Invited Experts (https://www.w3.org/invited-experts/)\n\n Resources (https://www.w3.org/resources/)\n\n Resources\n\n Master web fundamentals, use our developer tools, or contribute code.\n\n Learn from W3C resources (https://www.w3.org/resources/)\n\n Developers (https://www.w3.org/developers/)\n\n Validators & tools (https://www.w3.org/developers/tools/)\n\n Accessibility fundamentals (https://www.w3.org/WAI/fundamentals/)\n\n Internationalization (i18n) (https://www.w3.org/International/i18n-drafts/nav/about)\n\n Translations of W3C standards & drafts (https://www.w3.org/translations/)\n\n Code of conduct (https://www.w3.org/policies/code-of-conduct/)\n\n Reports (https://www.w3.org/reports/)\n\n News & events (https://www.w3.org/news-events/)\n\n News & events\n\n Recent content across news, blogs, press releases, media; upcoming events.\n\n Explore news & events (https://www.w3.org/news-events/)\n\n News (https://www.w3.org/news/)\n\n Blog (https://www.w3.org/blog/)\n\n Press releases (https://www.w3.org/press-releases/)\n\n Press & media (https://www.w3.org/about/press-media/)\n\n Events (https://www.w3.org/events/)\n\n Annual W3C Conference (TPAC) (https://www.w3.org/news-events/w3c-tpac/)\n\n Code of conduct (https://www.w3.org/policies/code-of-conduct/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Support us\n\n Make a huge difference to our operations as a public-interest non-profit, and help us to achieve our vision.\n\n Ways you can support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n About (https://www.w3.org/about/)\n\n About\n\n Understand our values and principles, learn our history, look into our policies, meet our people.\n\n Find out more about us (https://www.w3.org/about/)\n\n Our mission (https://www.w3.org/mission/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Leadership (https://www.w3.org/about/leadership/)\n\n Staff (https://www.w3.org/staff/)\n\n Careers (https://www.w3.org/careers/)\n\n Media kit (https://www.w3.org/about/press-media/)\n\n Diversity (https://www.w3.org/about/diversity/)\n\n Evangelists (https://www.w3.org/evangelists/)\n\n Corporation (https://www.w3.org/about/corporation/)\n\n Policies & legal information (https://www.w3.org/policies/)\n\n Contact us (https://www.w3.org/contact/)\n\n Help (https://www.w3.org/help/)\n\n Search (https://www.w3.org/help/search/)\n\n My account (https://www.w3.org/users/myprofile/)\n\n Home (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n Guidebook (https://www.w3.org/guide/)\n\n The Art of Consensus (Guidebook)\n\n Page contents\n\n Groups \n \n Incubation: Community Group and Workshops \n\n Starting a Group \n\n Running a Group \n\n Closing a Group \n\n Chairs \n\n Staff Contacts \n\n Specification Development \n \n Editors \n\n Advancing on the Recommendation Track \n\n Test Suites \n\n Other considerations \n\n Speaking about your work \n\n Advisory Committee \n\n Reference \n \n Guidelines and Policies \n\n Patent Policy \n\n About the Guidebook \n\n This Guidebook provides information for W3C participants about processes and practices that may affect their work at W3C. See About the Guidebook for details.\n\n Groups\n\n A variety of W3C Groups (https://www.w3.org/groups/) enable W3C to pursue its mission through the creation of Web standards, guidelines, and supporting materials.\n\n A list of group procedures related to the W3C Process is available.\n\n Incubation: Community Group and Workshops\n\n How to incubate (https://www.w3.org/guide/incubation.html)\n\n W3C Community & Business Groups (https://www.w3.org/community/)\n\n W3C Workshops (https://www.w3.org/guide/meetings/workshops.html) for starting incubation\n\n Exploration Interest Group (https://www.w3.org/groups/ig/exploration/)\n\n How to transition work from a Community Group to a Working Group (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/cg-transition.html)\n\n Considerations when transferring a CG Specification for Standardization (https://github.com/w3c/cg-council/blob/main/transfer-considerations.md)\n\n Starting a Group\n\n Recommendation Track Readiness Best Practices (https://www.w3.org/guide/standards-track/)\n\n How to create a Working Group or Interest Group Charter (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/charter.html)\n\n Tool: Chartering dashboard (https://www.w3.org/2024/03/charters-in-dev.html)\n\n How to set up a new group (https://www.w3.org/guide/tools/new-group.html)\n\n Running a Group\n\n Join a group (https://www.w3.org/get-involved/) (see also Invited Expert Policy (https://www.w3.org/invited-experts/))\n\n Resources related to:\n \n online and in-person meetings and workshops (https://www.w3.org/guide/meetings/)\n\n tools (https://www.w3.org/guide/tools/)\n\n the W3C Process \n\n People management\n \n HumanDimension (https://www.w3.org/wiki/Guide/HumanDimension) (a Chair training module)\n\n Code of Conduct (https://www.w3.org/policies/code-of-conduct/) and Procedures (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/coc-incident-resolution-ombuds.html) to assist all parties when issues arise.\n \n We recommend reminding your group about the CoC regularly. Suggested wording, “W3C operates under the Code of Conduct. The goal of this Code of Conduct is to ensure that W3C is an environment where everyone who participates is treated equitably and with respect.\n\n We have guidelines to suspend or remove participants from groups (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/suspension.html). See more on the Positive Work Environment Home Page (https://www.w3.org/about/positive-work-environment/)\n\n Check out our Working Groups (https://www.w3.org/PM/Groups/chairboards.html?gtype=working) and Interest Groups (https://www.w3.org/PM/Groups/chairboards.html?gtype=interest) dashboards to navigate through what we know about them. The Project Management team maintains a collection of links and tools (https://www.w3.org/PM/) to keep track.\n\n Closing a Group\n\n How to Close a Working Group (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/closing-wg.html)\n\n Chairs\n\n Chair’s role (https://www.w3.org/guide/chair/role.html)\n\n W3C Chairs home page (https://www.w3.org/guide/chair/)\n\n W3C Chair Buddy System (https://www.w3.org/guide/chair/buddy.html) (volunteer experienced Chairs can mentor other Chairs)\n\n Staff Contacts\n\n Staff Contact’s role (https://www.w3.org/guide/teamcontact/role.html)\n\n W3C Staff Contacts home page (https://www.w3.org/guide/teamcontact/)\n\n Liaison’s role (https://www.w3.org/guide/teamcontact/liaison-role.html). Note: Per section “Liaisons” of the Process Document, liaisons MUST be coordinated by the Team due to requirements for public communication; patent, copyright, and other IPR policies; confidentiality agreements; and mutual membership agreements.\n\n Specification Development\n\n Editors\n\n Editor’s role (https://www.w3.org/guide/editor/role.html), Editor, Author, Contributor Policies (https://www.w3.org/Signature/Contributor.html)\n\n W3C Editors home page (https://www.w3.org/guide/editor/) and specifically the Style for Group-internal Drafts (https://www.w3.org/guide/editor/editors-draft.html)\n\n Normative References (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/tilt/normative-references.html); considerations the Team takes into account when evaluating normative references\n\n W3C on GitHub (https://www.w3.org/guide/github/)\n \n Using GitHub for Spec Work (https://www.w3.org/guide/github/specs.html)\n\n Using GitHub for W3C specifications (slides) (https://www.w3.org/2015/Talks/1217-github-w3c/)\n\n GitHub Repository Manager (https://labs.w3.org/repo-manager/): One interface to find all group GitHub contributors and the IPR status\n\n PR Preview - Adds preview and diff links to pull requests (https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/spec-prod/2017JanMar/0047.html) (Config Helper (https://tobie.github.io/pr-preview/config.html))\n\n W3C Manual of Style (https://www.w3.org/guide/manual-of-style/)\n\n Advancing on the Recommendation Track\n\n Check the transition requirements (https://www.w3.org/guide/transitions/) for all W3C maturity levels (First Public Draft, CR, REC, etc.)\n \n next step finder (https://www.w3.org/guide/transitions/nextstep.html), if you’re not sure what the next state should be\n\n milestones calculator (https://www.w3.org/guide/transitions/milestones/), to help with dates.\n\n Predicting milestones (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/predicting-milestones.html).\n\n Request Wide Review (https://www.w3.org/guide/documentreview/) at least 2-3 months before CR , to allow time for discussion and rework. This includes requesting horizontal reviews .\n\n Addressing formal objections: best practices to resolve and decide Formal Objections (https://www.w3.org/guide/council/council.html) (aka council guide)\n\n Tips for getting to Recommendation faster (https://www.w3.org/guide/standards-track/rec-tips.html)\n\n Considerations on the final maturity stage of group deliverables (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/living-cr-rec.html)\n\n Submitting a W3C Recommendation to ISO: the PAS transposition process (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/pas-transposition-process.html)\n\n Test Suites\n\n Start with web-platform-tests (https://web-platform-tests.org/) (WPT), home for the Open Web test suites (including W3C ones).\n\n All WPT contributions are licensed under the terms of the 3-Clause BSD License (https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/blob/master/LICENSE.md). For non-WPT test suites, see Test suites licenses (https://www.w3.org/copyright/test-suites-licenses/) for Contribution of Test Cases\n\n …more questions about the testing infrastructure? Contact public-test-infra@w3.org (https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/public-test-infra/)\n\n Other considerations\n\n Obsoleting and Rescinding W3C Specifications (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/obsolete-rescinded-supserseded.html)\n\n Member Submissions (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/member-submission.html)\n\n Copyrights for documents, software, tests (https://www.w3.org/copyright/)\n\n Considerations for joint deliverables (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/joint-deliverables.html)\n\n W3C Documents and license related to API definitions, code samples, or examples (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/binding-license.html)\n\n W3C Standards Vulnerability Disclosure & Handling Process and Policy (https://www.w3.org/TR/security-disclosure/)\n\n Discussion about specifications tooling and versioning on spec-prod@w3.org (https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/spec-prod/)\n\n Speaking about your work\n\n Blogs, articles, talks, meetups (https://www.w3.org/news-events/about-w3c-events/), Press interviews: Work Group participants, TAG Members, AB Members, W3C Evangelists or Chapters, W3C Staff are among the world’s experts in web technologies and their impacts. These are coordinated to ensure a cohesive coordination across the Consortium, that applicable meeting requirements are met, that using the W3C Brand is done according to expectations. Give heads-up , share relevant work, things you author, or coordinate press enquiries, by writing to the W3C Communications team <w3t-pr@w3.org> about how you may attribute your work (or not) to W3C.\n\n We have a slide template (https://www.w3.org/Talks/Templates/b6plus-slideset.html) we encourage people to use.\n\n Write on the W3C Blog (https://www.w3.org/news-events/about-w3c-blog/) (open to W3C Group participants, members of the W3C Team); talks publicized on W3C home page (https://www.w3.org/), W3C Social Media.\n\n Press release testimonial guidelines (https://www.w3.org/2004/12/testimonial_pr-guidelines.html)\n\n Creating and delivering effective presentations (https://www.w3.org/wiki/Speaker_Resources)\n\n Making Events Accessible (https://www.w3.org/WAI/teach-advocate/accessible-presentations/)\n\n AB and TAG: Guidelines for communicating as a member of a W3C elected body (https://www.w3.org/guide/other/elected-body-communication-guidelines.html)\n\n Advisory Committee\n\n Resources for Advisory Committee representatives \n\n Formal Objections & W3C Council (https://www.w3.org/guide/council/)\n\n Reference\n\n Guidelines and Policies\n\n Policies & legal information (https://www.w3.org/policies/)\n\n Positive Work Environment at W3C: Code of Conduct (https://www.w3.org/policies/code-of-conduct/)\n\n Antitrust and Competition Guidance (https://www.w3.org/policies/antitrust/)\n\n Policy Regarding Non-Disclosure Agreements and W3C Meetings (https://www.w3.org/policies/no-nda/)\n\n Charter Extensions (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/charter-extensions.html)\n\n Invited Experts policy (https://www.w3.org/invited-experts/)\n\n Normative References guidelines (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/tilt/normative-references.html)\n\n Processing of Formal Objections (https://www.w3.org/guide/council/council.html)\n\n Continuity of Operations under Travel Restrictions (https://www.w3.org/guide/meetings/continuity.html)\n\n Patent Policy\n\n W3C Patent Policy (https://www.w3.org/policies/patent-policy/)\n\n Commentary: FAQ (https://www.w3.org/2003/12/22-pp-faq.html), Summary (https://www.w3.org/2004/02/05-patentsummary.html), and Business Benefits (https://www.w3.org/2004/03/pp-points-20040210.html)\n\n Patent Policy Information about Groups and Disclosures can be found from the Group pages (https://www.w3.org/groups/)\n\n …more questions? Contact the PSIG (https://www.w3.org/2004/pp/psig/)\n\n About the Guidebook\n\n Got a question? Join #general in IRC (https://webirc.w3.org/?channels=general) or Slack (https://w3ccommunity.slack.com/) (invite (https://www.w3.org/slack-w3ccommunity-invite)). \n\n This Guidebook is intended to complement the W3C Membership Agreement (https://www.w3.org/Consortium/Agreement/Appendix) and the W3C Process (https://www.w3.org/policies/process/). This page is Public , although a small number of resources linked from this page may be visible only to the W3C Membership or Team.\n\n You are expected to be familiar with the parts of this Guidebook that affect your work. Working Group chairs should get a “tour” from their Staff Contact. Then take a look again, for example, if you’re going to hold a face-to-face meeting; read the section on meetings to be sure you understand what’s written there, and to record any valuable knowledge you pick up along the way.\n\n As editor of the guidebook, @w3c/guidebook (https://github.com/orgs/w3c/teams/guidebook) will do its best to see that it gets better over time. This does not mean that we do all the editing ourselves!\n\n Related content\n\n Guidebook (https://www.w3.org/guide/)\n\n Process (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/)\n\n Code of Conduct (https://www.w3.org/policies/code-of-conduct/)\n\n Policies & legal (https://www.w3.org/policies/)\n\n This document lives in GitHub (https://github.com/w3c/guide/), where changes can be tracked and pull requests are welcome. Feedback and comments are welcome. Please use GitHub issues (https://github.com/w3c/guide/issues). \n\n Home (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n Contact (https://www.w3.org/contact/)\n\n Help (https://www.w3.org/help/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Legal & Policies (https://www.w3.org/policies/)\n\n Corporation (https://www.w3.org/about/corporation/)\n\n System Status (https://status.w3.org/)\n\n W3C on Mastodon (https://w3c.social/@w3c)\n\n W3C on GitHub (https://github.com/w3c/)\n\n Copyright &copy; 2026 World Wide Web Consortium (https://www.w3.org/).\n W3C &reg; liability , trademark and permissive license (https://www.w3.org/copyright/document-license/) rules apply.','9417dab4602d88126b300272be4b1bd1f438054fda796056e6f2ba29e047f397','W/\"6abfcd1f-8ddd\"','Fri, 02 Oct 2026 15:26:23 GMT','2026-10-05 07:46:09','INDEXED','2026-10-05 07:41:28','2026-10-05 07:46:09'),(878,7,36,'218bd0da026f0167501452cf7294ad24958b9c5a103b47f9bd3d53e753ffae6e','https://www.w3.org/about/positive-work-environment/','Positive Work Environment | About us | W3C','Positive Work Environment | About us | W3C \n\n Skip to content \n\n 日本語ホームページJapanese website (https://www.w3.org/ja/)\n\n 简体中文首页Chinese website (https://www.w3.org/zh-hans/)\n\n Visit the W3C homepage (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n Standards & groups (https://www.w3.org/standards/)\n\n Standards & groups\n\n Understand the various specifications, their maturity levels on the web standards track, their adoption, and the groups that develop them.\n\n Explore web standards (https://www.w3.org/standards/)\n\n About W3C web standards (https://www.w3.org/standards/about/)\n\n W3C standards & drafts (https://www.w3.org/TR/)\n\n Types of documents W3C publishes (https://www.w3.org/standards/types/)\n\n W3C groups (https://www.w3.org/groups/)\n\n Translations of W3C standards & drafts (https://www.w3.org/translations/)\n\n Reviews & public feedback (https://www.w3.org/standards/review/)\n\n Promote web standards (https://www.w3.org/get-involved/promote/)\n\n Liaisons (https://www.w3.org/liaisons/)\n\n Technical Architecture Group (TAG) (https://www.w3.org/groups/other/tag/)\n\n Get involved (https://www.w3.org/get-involved/)\n\n Get involved\n\n W3C works at the nexus of core technology, industry needs, and societal needs.\n\n Find ways to get involved (https://www.w3.org/get-involved/)\n\n Browse our work by industry (https://www.w3.org/ecosystems/)\n\n Become a Member (https://www.w3.org/membership/)\n\n Member home (restricted) (https://www.w3.org/Member/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Mailing lists (https://www.w3.org/email/)\n\n Participant guidebook (https://www.w3.org/guide/)\n\n Positive work environment (https://www.w3.org/about/positive-work-environment/)\n\n Invited Experts (https://www.w3.org/invited-experts/)\n\n Resources (https://www.w3.org/resources/)\n\n Resources\n\n Master web fundamentals, use our developer tools, or contribute code.\n\n Learn from W3C resources (https://www.w3.org/resources/)\n\n Developers (https://www.w3.org/developers/)\n\n Validators & tools (https://www.w3.org/developers/tools/)\n\n Accessibility fundamentals (https://www.w3.org/WAI/fundamentals/)\n\n Internationalization (i18n) (https://www.w3.org/International/i18n-drafts/nav/about)\n\n Translations of W3C standards & drafts (https://www.w3.org/translations/)\n\n Code of conduct (https://www.w3.org/policies/code-of-conduct/)\n\n Reports (https://www.w3.org/reports/)\n\n News & events (https://www.w3.org/news-events/)\n\n News & events\n\n Recent content across news, blogs, press releases, media; upcoming events.\n\n Explore news & events (https://www.w3.org/news-events/)\n\n News (https://www.w3.org/news/)\n\n Blog (https://www.w3.org/blog/)\n\n Press releases (https://www.w3.org/press-releases/)\n\n Press & media (https://www.w3.org/about/press-media/)\n\n Events (https://www.w3.org/events/)\n\n Annual W3C Conference (TPAC) (https://www.w3.org/news-events/tpac/)\n\n Code of conduct (https://www.w3.org/policies/code-of-conduct/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Support us\n\n Make a huge difference to our operations as a public-interest non-profit, and help us to achieve our vision.\n\n Ways you can support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n About (https://www.w3.org/about/)\n\n About\n\n Understand our values and principles, learn our history, look into our policies, meet our people.\n\n Find out more about us (https://www.w3.org/about/)\n\n Our mission (https://www.w3.org/mission/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Leadership (https://www.w3.org/about/leadership/)\n\n Staff (https://www.w3.org/staff/)\n\n Careers (https://www.w3.org/careers/)\n\n Media kit (https://www.w3.org/about/press-media/)\n\n Diversity (https://www.w3.org/about/diversity/)\n\n Corporation (https://www.w3.org/about/corporation/)\n\n Policies & legal information (https://www.w3.org/policies/)\n\n Contact us (https://www.w3.org/contact/)\n\n Help (https://www.w3.org/help/)\n\n Search (https://www.w3.org/help/search/)\n\n My account (https://www.w3.org/users/myprofile/)\n\n Home (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n About us (https://www.w3.org/about/)\n\n Positive Work Environment (https://www.w3.org/about/positive-work-environment/)\n\n Positive Work Environment\n\n Read this page in: \n\n 中文（简体） (https://www.w3.org/zh-hans/about/positive-work-environment/)\n\n 日本語 (https://www.w3.org/ja/about/positive-work-environment/)\n\n Page contents\n\n Education and Training\n\n Feedback and Status\n\n Acknowledgements\n\n W3C is a global community where participants choose to work together. In that community, we experience differences in language, location, nationality, and experience. In such a diverse environment, misunderstandings and disagreements happen, and in most cases can be resolved informally.\n\n W3C has developed several resources:\n\n W3C Code of Conduct (Code) (https://www.w3.org/policies/code-of-conduct/), which defines a set of community principles regarding how people working in the W3C ecosystem should treat each other.\n\n Procedures (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/coc-incident-resolution-ombuds.html), to assist all parties when there are issues that arise.\n\n We also curate a list of Education and training materials.\n\n Education and Training\n\n Several resources are available:\n\n Netiquette guidelines (https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc1855)\n\n Codes of Conduct 101 + FAQ (https://www.ashedryden.com/blog/codes-of-conduct-101-faq)\n\n Geek Feminism Conference anti-harassment/Policy (https://geekfeminism.fandom.com/wiki/Conference_anti-harassment/Policy)\n\n Feedback and Status\n\n Feedback\n\n The Positive Work Environment Community Group (https://www.w3.org/community/pwe/) is responsible for the evolution of this document.\n\n Send comments or questions on this document to public-pwe@w3.org [publicly archived (https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/public-pwe/)]. Note: Do not use this list to report inappropriate behavior; refer to Procedures (https://www.w3.org/guide/process/coc-incident-resolution-ombuds.html).\n\n Acknowledgements\n\n We acknowledge the contributions from the participants in the former Positive Work Environment Task Force:\n\n Kazuyuki Ashimura, Ann Bassetti (former Chair), Steve Bratt, Beau Brendler, Judy Brewer, Daniel Dardailler (former Chair and editor), Ted Guild, Simon Hernandez, Jeffrey Jaffe (former W3C CEO), Coralie Mercier (former Chair and editor), Charles Nevile, Mauro Nunez, Antonio Olmo Titos, Florian Rivoal, Tzviya Siegman, Jeanne Spellman, Wendy Seltzer, Ralph Swick, Amy van der Hiel (former Chair), Steve Zilles. As well as all those who provided input for improvements over time. \n\n Coralie Mercier (https://www.w3.org/People/CMercier/), Head of W3C Marketing & Communications\n\n In this section\n\n Corporation (https://www.w3.org/about/corporation/)\n\n Diversity (https://www.w3.org/about/diversity/)\n\n Functional organization (https://www.w3.org/about/functional-organization/)\n\n History (https://www.w3.org/about/history/)\n\n Leadership (https://www.w3.org/about/leadership/)\n\n Media kit (https://www.w3.org/about/press-media/)\n\n Positive Work Environment (https://www.w3.org/about/positive-work-environment/)\n\n W3C Council (https://www.w3.org/about/council/)\n\n Home (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n Contact (https://www.w3.org/contact/)\n\n Help (https://www.w3.org/help/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Legal & Policies (https://www.w3.org/policies/)\n\n Corporation (https://www.w3.org/about/corporation/)\n\n System Status (https://status.w3.org/)\n\n W3C on Mastodon (https://w3c.social/@w3c)\n\n W3C on GitHub (https://github.com/w3c/)\n\n Copyright &copy; 2026 World Wide Web Consortium (https://www.w3.org/).\n W3C &reg; liability , trademark and permissive license (https://www.w3.org/copyright/document-license/) rules apply.','7e8bb267a2df9810be