vited-experts/)\n\n Resources (https://www.w3.org/resources/)\n\n Resources\n\n Master web fundamentals, use our developer tools, or contribute code.\n\n Learn from W3C resources (https://www.w3.org/resources/)\n\n Developers (https://www.w3.org/developers/)\n\n Validators & tools (https://www.w3.org/developers/tools/)\n\n Accessibility fundamentals (https://www.w3.org/WAI/fundamentals/)\n\n Internationalization (i18n) (https://www.w3.org/International/i18n-drafts/nav/about)\n\n Translations of W3C standards & drafts (https://www.w3.org/translations/)\n\n Code of conduct (https://www.w3.org/policies/code-of-conduct/)\n\n Reports (https://www.w3.org/reports/)\n\n News & events (https://www.w3.org/news-events/)\n\n News & events\n\n Recent content across news, blogs, press releases, media; upcoming events.\n\n Explore news & events (https://www.w3.org/news-events/)\n\n News (https://www.w3.org/news/)\n\n Blog (https://www.w3.org/blog/)\n\n Press releases (https://www.w3.org/press-releases/)\n\n Press & media (https://www.w3.org/about/press-media/)\n\n Events (https://www.w3.org/events/)\n\n Annual W3C Conference (TPAC) (https://www.w3.org/news-events/tpac/)\n\n Code of conduct (https://www.w3.org/policies/code-of-conduct/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Support us\n\n Make a huge difference to our operations as a public-interest non-profit, and help us to achieve our vision.\n\n Ways you can support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n About (https://www.w3.org/about/)\n\n About\n\n Understand our values and principles, learn our history, look into our policies, meet our people.\n\n Find out more about us (https://www.w3.org/about/)\n\n Our mission (https://www.w3.org/mission/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Leadership (https://www.w3.org/about/leadership/)\n\n Staff (https://www.w3.org/staff/)\n\n Careers (https://www.w3.org/careers/)\n\n Media kit (https://www.w3.org/about/press-media/)\n\n Diversity (https://www.w3.org/about/diversity/)\n\n Corporation (https://www.w3.org/about/corporation/)\n\n Policies & legal information (https://www.w3.org/policies/)\n\n Contact us (https://www.w3.org/contact/)\n\n Help (https://www.w3.org/help/)\n\n Search (https://www.w3.org/help/search/)\n\n My account (https://www.w3.org/users/myprofile/)\n\n Home (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n Invited Experts (https://www.w3.org/invited-experts/)\n\n Invited Experts\n\n Read this page in: \n\n 中文（简体） (https://www.w3.org/zh-hans/invited-experts/)\n\n 日本語 (https://www.w3.org/ja/invited-experts/)\n\n Page contents\n\n Introduction\n\n Principles guiding invitations\n\n Instructions\n\n Terms\n\n Member access to the W3C website\n\n Review\n\n Invited Expert identity program\n\n Invited Experts support fund\n\n Becoming a W3C Member\n\n Introduction\n\n W3C Invited Experts are individuals who are invited by a chair of a Working or Interest Group to participate in that group. The chair and the W3C staff must agree that the individual brings particular expertise to the group prior to inviting the individual.\n\n Principles guiding invitations\n\n Section 3.4.3.3 of the W3C Process Document explains that W3C sometimes invites an individual with particular expertise to become a Invited Expert in a Group . The Team generally follows these principles when considering an application:\n\n Invited Expert status is normally granted to either independent individuals (i.e., individuals not significantly affiliated with business interests), or to academics affiliated with institutions of higher learning.\n\n Invited Expert status is not normally granted to individuals employed by organizations which have significant business interest in results from W3C. This might even include some not-for-profit organizations. Such organizations SHOULD join W3C (https://www.w3.org/membership/).\n\n Under almost no circumstances will Invited Expert status be granted to an individual whose support comes from a financially solvent organization which has terminated its W3C membership.\n\n If the case is made that granting Invited Expert status to an individual supported by a \"membership candidate\" organization would enhance the chances that organization would join W3C, then a provisional 3-month or 6-month invitation might be extended.\n\n Instructions\n\n An individual becomes an Invited Expert in a group as follows:\n\n The Chair(s) and Team Contact hold preliminary discussions with the individual.\n\n The individual provides the information required of an Invited Expert:\n\n If you do not already have a W3C Account (check if there is an account associated with your e-mail address (https://www.w3.org/account/recover/)), complete the account request form (https://www.w3.org/account/request/).\n\n After you receive your W3C login name and password, find the Working Group (https://www.w3.org/groups/wg/) or Interest Group (https://www.w3.org/groups/ig/) you want to join. Select this group, click the \"How to join\" link and follow instructions from the \"If you do not work for a W3C Member organization\" section.\n\n The Team Contact reviews the request, with internal additional review when W3C Member access needs to be granted.\n\n If the application is approved, the Team invites the individual to complete the procedure of formally joining the Group.\n\n Terms\n\n All Invited Experts are invited to participate for the terms of the current Charter(s) for the group(s) they are joining, except that provisional invitations granted to “membership candidate” organizations may have shorter terms. Additionally, Chairs may in exceptional circumstances give Invited Experts a term shorter than the remaining charter duration.\n\n Member access to the W3C website\n\n Invited Experts are granted W3C Member access by default. At the discretion of the Team Contact, or at the request of the invited expert themselves, W3C Member access may be not granted. This option accounts for the Invited Experts who do not wish to be responsible for managing member-confidential information, or are unable to.\n\n Additionally, on a case by case basis, a group may define in its charter that invitations to Invited Experts will not include W3C Member access. The Team may consult with the W3C Advisory Board about when such restrictions are appropriate.\n\n Review\n\n When a Charter expires, Chairs and Team Contacts must explicitly review all Invited Experts within that group. If a Group is allowed to continue meeting after the Charter has expired, the Invited Experts in that group will also be extended until the group is formally closed or the Charter is renewed. The Chairs and Team Contacts should determine if the Invited Expert’s expertise is still needed based on the scope of any new Charter. As part of the review, if the Invited Expert has stopped actively contributing to the group, their Invited Expert status should be terminated by the Chairs and Team Contact of the group.\n\n The status of all Invited Experts may be reviewed more frequently, to ensure that information supplied on their application is still current (e.g., organizational affiliation, participation commitment, etc.)\n\n In the event that an Invited Expert\'s circumstances have changed, such as a change of affiliation, Chairs and Team Contacts may decide to terminate their invitation.\n\n W3C Management reserves the right to discontinue an individual\'s Invited Expert status at any time.\n\n Invited Expert identity program\n\n Public directory\n\n The public W3C Invited Experts directory (https://www.w3.org/invited-experts/list/) exposes a combination of W3C database information and information Invited Experts may enter via their W3C profile (https://www.w3.org/users/myprofile/):\n\n First and Last Name\n\n Group(s) in which each Invited Expert is invited\n\n Photo\n\n GitHub icon linking to Github profile\n\n Title\n\n Biography\n\n Note: Except the first two items, any information above may be customized and updated via the Invited Expert\'s W3C profile (https://www.w3.org/users/myprofile/).\n\n Restricted mailing list\n\n W3C Invited Experts can interact via group-invited-experts@w3.org (https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Group/group-invited-experts/), a restricted archived mailing list. Subscription and unsubscription is automatic based on Invited Expert status. The web archive is available only to Members of the Invited Expert group and the W3C Team.\n\n Note: In the absence of opt-out, individuals may rely on local mail filters.\n\n Designated observer at W3C AC Meetings\n\n To help W3C Invited Experts organize as a group of disparate individuals who may want to use a united voice to speak about some topic or other, W3C Invited Experts may designate one individual amongst themselves to attend as an observer the W3C Advisory Committee Meetings which take place twice a year. That non-voting individual represents the interests of W3C Invited Experts and is allowed to summarize and bring back information, keeping in mind that AC Meetings are member-confidential.\n\n To that effect, a straw-man proposal is for the designated Invited Expert to self-declare to ie-contact@w3.org who subscribes them to w3c-ac-forum@w3.org, the member-only archived (https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Member/w3c-ac-forum/) mailing list for discussion among W3C Advisory Committee Members and the Advisory Board on matters of relevance to W3C.\n\n Invited Experts support fund\n\n The Invited Experts support fund aims to help reduce barriers for Invited Experts who have shown their commitment through positive contributions as Chairs, Editors and/or elected/appointed member of the TAG and/or the AB, but that require support to be more actively involved to contribute more frequently, widely or in a meaningful way at TPAC, AC meeting and/or group meetings.\n\n The Invited Experts support initiative contributes to W3C stakeholder strategy, as outlined in the 2025-2028 Strategic Objectives and Initiatives (https://www.w3.org/2025/06/w3c-2025-2028-strategic-objectives-and-initiatives/index.html).\n\n The amount available for the fund is based on W3C member dues, funding from the Community Engagement Champions sponsorship plus donations (https://www.w3.org/donate/). We encourage you to contribute generously.\n\n The fund will be open to Invited Experts that:\n\n have received the Invited Expert status at least six months prior to the call for funding applications, and attended at least one meeting prior to the application\n\n have a role as Chairs, Editors and/or elected/appointed TAG or AB members\n\n have a good track record of attendance to group(s) meetings and uphold the W3C Code of Conduct, meaning that they are participants in good standing\n\n require financial support for TPAC and/or other events planned prior to the end of the calendar year.\n\n Please read more in the separate Invited Experts support fund page (https://www.w3.org/invited-experts/ie-support-fund/).\n\n Becoming a W3C Member\n\n Becoming W3C Members (https://www.w3.org/membership/) benefits the overall work that W3C does, and in doing so, W3C Members drive the direction of core web technology and exchange ideas with industry and research leaders .\n\n In the event that your circumstances have changed, such as an increase in the size of your organization, please consider transitioning by becoming a W3C Member. You may get in touch with the Membership Team.\n\n Related pages\n\n Invited Experts support fund (https://www.w3.org/invited-experts/ie-support-fund/)\n\n This page describes the criteria and process for W3C Invited Experts to apply for support funding.\n\n Page\n\n Home (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n Contact (https://www.w3.org/contact/)\n\n Help (https://www.w3.org/help/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/support-us/)\n\n Legal & Policies (https://www.w3.org/policies/)\n\n Corporation (https://www.w3.org/about/corporation/)\n\n System Status (https://status.w3.org/)\n\n W3C on Mastodon (https://w3c.social/@w3c)\n\n W3C on GitHub (https://github.com/w3c/)\n\n Copyright &copy; 2026 World Wide Web Consortium (https://www.w3.org/).\n W3C &reg; liability , trademark and permissive license (https://www.w3.org/copyright/document-license/) rules apply.','2e548aaecb398ecfdce482639f01ce84abe0d39474a244b076381ff815df4692',NULL,NULL,'2026-10-05 07:46:09','INDEXED','2026-10-05 07:41:28','2026-10-05 07:46:09'),(880,7,36,'218bd0da026f0167501452cf7294ad24958b9c5a103b47f9bd3d53e753ffae6e','https://www.w3.org/resources/','Resources | W3C','Resources | W3C \n\n Skip to content \n\n 日本語ホームページJapanese website (https://www.w3.org/ja/)\n\n 简体中文首页Chinese website (https://www.w3.org/zh-hans/)\n\n Visit the W3C homepage (https://www.w3.org/)\n\n Standards & groups (https://www.w3.org/standards/)\n\n Standards & groups\n\n Understand the various specifications, their maturity levels on the web standards track, their adoption, and the groups that develop them.\n\n Explore web standards (https://www.w3.org/standards/)\n\n About W3C web standards (https://www.w3.org/standards/about/)\n\n W3C standards & drafts (https://www.w3.org/TR/)\n\n Types of documents W3C publishes (https://www.w3.org/standards/types/)\n\n W3C groups (https://www.w3.org/groups/)\n\n Translations of W3C standards & drafts (https://www.w3.org/translations/)\n\n Reviews & public feedback (https://www.w3.org/standards/review/)\n\n Promote web standards (https://www.w3.org/get-involved/promote/)\n\n Liaisons (https://www.w3.org/liaisons/)\n\n Technical Architecture Group (TAG) (https://www.w3.org/groups/other/tag/)\n\n Get involved (https://www.w3.org/get-involved/)\n\n Get involved\n\n W3C works at the nexus of core technology, industry needs, and societal needs.\n\n Find ways to get involved (https://www.w3.org/get-involved/)\n\n Browse our work by industry (https://www.w3.org/ecosystems/)\n\n Become a Member (https://www.w3.org/membership/)\n\n Member home (restricted) (https://www.w3.org/Member/)\n\n Support us (https://www.w3.org/supp