://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_getstarted.php)\n\n Basic Commands\n\nBash Commands (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_commands.php)\nBash List (ls) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_ls.php)\nBash Change Dir (cd) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_cd.php)\nBash Print Dir (pwd) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_pwd.php)\nBash Echo (echo) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_echo.php)\nBash Concatenate (cat) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_cat.php)\nBash Copy (cp) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_cp.php)\nBash Move (mv) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_mv.php)\nBash Remove (rm) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_rm.php)\nBash Timestamp (touch) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_touch.php)\nBash Make Dir (mkdir) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_mkdir.php)\nBash Manual (man) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_man.php)\nBash Alias (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_alias.php)\n\n Text Processing\n\nBash Search Text (grep) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_grep.php)\nBash Pattern Scan (awk) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_awk.php)\nBash Stream Editor (sed) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_sed.php)\nBash Remove Section (cut) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_cut.php)\nBash Sort Lines (sort) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_sort.php)\nBash View End (tail) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_tail.php)\nBash View Start (head) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_head.php)\n\n System Monitoring\n\nBash Process Status (ps) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_ps.php)\nBash List Processes (top) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_top.php)\nBash Disk Space (df) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_df.php)\nBash Directory Usage (du) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_du.php)\nBash Memory Usage (free) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_free.php)\nBash Terminate (kill) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_kill.php)\nBash Uptime (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_uptime.php)\n\n Networking\n\nBash Ping (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_ping.php)\nBash URL Transfer (curl) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_curl.php)\nBash Downloader (wget) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_wget.php)\nBash Remote Connect (ssh) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_ssh.php)\nBash Secure Copy (scp) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_scp.php)\nBash File Sync (rsync) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_rsync.php)\n\n File Compression\n\nBash Compress (zip) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_zip.php)\nBash Extract (unzip) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_unzip.php)\nBash TAR Archive (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_tar.php)\n\n File Permissions\n\nBash Ownership (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_permissions.php)\nBash Modify (chmod) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_chmod.php)\nBash Ownership (chown) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_chown.php)\nBash Group (chgrp) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_chgrp.php)\n\n Scripting\n\nBash Syntax (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_syntax.php)\nBash Script (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_script.php)\nBash Variables (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_variables.php)\nBash Data Types (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_data_types.php)\nBash Operators (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_operators.php)\nBash If...Else (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_conditions.php)\nBash Loops (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_loops.php)\nBash Functions (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_functions.php)\nBash Arrays (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_arrays.php)\nBash Schedule (cron) (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_cron.php)\n\n Bash Cert\n\nBash Certificate (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_exam.php)\n\n Exercises and Quiz\n\nBash Exercises (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_exercises.php)\nBash Quiz (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_quiz.php)\n\n Bash Tutorial \n\n❮ Home (https://www.w3schools.com/default.asp)\nNext ❯ (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_intro.php)\n\n Learn Bash\n\n Bash is used to write scripts and run commands on Linux systems.\n\n It helps automate tasks, manage system operations, and boost productivity.\n\n Learn Bash now ❯ (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_intro.php)\n\n Become Bash Certified\n\n Get certified with our Bash exam, includes a professionally curated study kit to guide you from beginner to exam-ready.\n\nGet Certified ❯ (https://campus.w3schools.com/collections/certifications/products/bash-certification-exam)\n\n Share \n\n Link Copied \n\n Understanding Shells\n\n A shell is a text-based interface that lets you talk to your computer.\n\n There are different types of shells, but Bash (Bourne Again SHell) is the most popular because it\'s powerful and easy to use.\n\n Types of Shells: \n\n Bourne Shell (sh): The original Unix shell, developed by Stephen Bourne.\n\n C Shell (csh): Known for its C-like syntax, popular for interactive use.\n\n Korn Shell (ksh): Combines features of sh and csh, offering advanced scripting capabilities.\n\n Bash (Bourne Again SHell): An improved version of sh, with additional features like command history and tab completion.\n\n Why Use Bash? \n\n It is widely available on Unix/Linux systems, making scripts portable.\n\n Supports powerful scripting features, including loops, conditionals, and functions.\n\n Provides command history and tab completion for ease of use.\n\n Can be integrated with other Unix/Linux tools for automation.\n\n Learning by Examples\n\n In this tutorial, we will show you Bash commands like this:\n\n Example\n\n bash --version\nGNU bash, version 5.2.21(1)-release (x86_64-pc-linux-gnu)\nCopyright (C) 2022 Free Software Foundation, Inc.\nLicense GPLv3+: GNU GPL version 3 or later\n\nThis is free software; you are free to change and redistribute it.\nThere is NO WARRANTY, to the extent permitted by law. \n\n For new users, using the terminal view can seem a bit complicated.\n\n Don\'t worry! We will keep it really simple, and learning this way gives you a good grasp of how Bash works.\n\n In the code above, you can see commands (input) and output.\n\n Lines like this are commands we input:\n\n Example\n\n bash --version \n\n Lines like this are the output/response to our commands:\n\n Example\n\n GNU bash, version 5.2.21(1)-release (x86_64-pc-linux-gnu)\nCopyright (C) 2022 Free Software Foundation, Inc.\nLicense GPLv3+: GNU GPL version 3 or later\n\nThis is free software; you are free to change and redistribute it.\nThere is NO WARRANTY, to the extent permitted by law. \n\n In general, lines with $ in front of it is input. These are the commands you can copy and run in your terminal.\n\n Bash Exercises\n\n Many chapters in this tutorial end with an exercise where you can check your level of knowledge.\n\n See all Bash Exercises (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_exercises.php)\n\n Bash Quiz\n\n Learn by taking a quiz! The quiz will give you a signal of how much you know about Bash.\n\n Start Bash Quiz (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_quiz.php)\n\n Track Your Progress\n\n Create a W3Schools account and get access to more features and learning materials:\n\n View your completed tutorials, exercises, and quizzes\n\n Keep an eye on your progress and daily streaks\n\n Join the leaderboard and compete with others\n\n Get your own avatar and unlock new skins\n\n Create your own personal website\n\n Sign Up &raquo; (https://profile.w3schools.com/sign-up)\n\n Note\n\n This is an optional feature. You can study at W3Schools without creating an account.\n\n❮ Home (https://www.w3schools.com/default.asp)\nNext ❯ (https://www.w3schools.com/bash/bash_intro.php)\n\n ★\n\n +1 (https://profile.w3schools.com/log-in?redirect_url=https%3A%2F%2Fmy-learning.w3schools.com)\n\n Sign in to track progress (https://profile.w3schools.com/log-in?redirect_url=https%3A%2F%2Fpathfinder.w3schools.com)\n\n REMOVE ADS (https://order.w3schools.com/plans)\n\n Learn more about Adventure Mode&rsaquo; (https://www.w3schools.com/adventure/)\n\n Free account\n\n Track your progress\n\n XP earned