enges_video.asp)\n\nHTML Audio (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_audio.asp)\n \n Audio (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_audio.asp)\n Code Challenge (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_challenges_audio.asp)\n\nHTML Plug-ins (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_object.asp)\nHTML YouTube (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_youtube.asp)\n\n Web APIs\n\nWeb APIs (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_api_whatis.asp)\nGeolocation API (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_geolocation.asp)\nDrag and Drop API (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_draganddrop.asp)\nPopover API (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_popover.asp)\nWeb Storage API (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_webstorage.asp)\nWeb Workers API (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_webworkers.asp)\nServer-Sent Events API (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_serversentevents.asp)\n\n HTML Cert\n\nHTML Certificate (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_exam.asp)\n\n HTML Examples\n\nHTML Examples (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_examples.asp)\nHTML Editor (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_editor.asp)\nHTML Quiz (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_quiz.asp)\nHTML Exercises (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_exercises.asp)\nHTML Challenges (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_challenges.asp)\nHTML Website (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_website.asp)\nHTML Syllabus (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_syllabus.asp)\nHTML Study Plan (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_study_plan.asp)\nHTML Interview Prep (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_interview_prep.asp)\nHTML Summary (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_summary.asp)\nHTML Accessibility (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_accessibility.asp)\n\n HTML References\n\nHTML Tag List (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/default.asp)\nHTML Attributes (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_attributes.asp)\nHTML Global Attributes (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_standardattributes.asp)\nHTML Browser Support (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_html_browsersupport.asp)\nHTML Events (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_eventattributes.asp)\nHTML Colors (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_colornames.asp)\nHTML Canvas (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_canvas.asp)\nHTML Audio/Video (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_av_dom.asp)\nHTML Doctypes (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_html_dtd.asp)\nHTML Character Sets (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_charactersets.asp)\nHTML URL Encode (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_urlencode.asp)\nHTML Lang Codes (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_language_codes.asp)\nHTTP Messages (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_httpmessages.asp)\nHTTP Methods (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_httpmethods.asp)\nPX to EM Converter (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_pxtoemconversion.asp)\nKeyboard Shortcuts (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_keyboardshortcuts.asp)\n\n HTML Headings \n\n❮ Previous (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_challenges_attributes.asp)\nNext ❯ (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_exercise_embed.asp?topic=headings)\n\n HTML headings are titles or subtitles that you want to display on a webpage.\n\n Example\n\n Heading 1\n\n Share \n\n Link Copied \n\n Heading 2\n\n Heading 3\n\n Heading 4\n\n Heading 5\n\n Heading 6\n\nTry it Yourself &raquo; (https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp?filename=tryhtml_headings)\n\n HTML Headings\n\n HTML headings are defined with the <h1> to <h6> tags.\n\n <h1> defines the most important heading. <h6> defines the least important heading.\n\n Example\n\n<h1>Heading 1</h1>\n\n<h2>Heading 2</h2>\n\n<h3>Heading 3</h3>\n<h4>Heading 4</h4>\n<h5>Heading 5</h5>\n<h6>Heading 6</h6>\n\nTry it Yourself &raquo; (https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp?filename=tryhtml_headings)\n\n Note: Browsers automatically add some white space (a margin) before and after a heading.\n\n Headings Are Important\n\n Search engines use the headings to index the structure and content of your web pages.\n\n Users often skim a page by its headings. It is important to use headings to show the document structure.\n\n <h1> headings should be used for main headings, followed by <h2> headings, then the less important \n <h3> , and so on.\n\n For example:\n\n <h1> - Page title\n\n <h2> - Section titles\n\n <h3> - Sub-sections\n\n Example\n\n<h1>Travel Guide</h1>\n\n<h2>Europe</h2>\n\n<h3>France</h3>\n\n<h3>Italy</h3>\n\n<h2>Asia</h2>\n\n<h3>India</h3>\n\n<h3>Thailand</h3>\n\nTry it Yourself &raquo; (https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp?filename=tryhtml_headings_structure)\n\n Tip: Use only one <h1> per page - it represents the main topic or title.\n\n Note: Use HTML headings for headings only. Don\'t use headings to make text \n BIG or bold .\n\n Bigger Headings\n\n Each HTML heading has a default size. However, you can specify the size for any heading \nwith the style attribute, using the CSS font-size property:\n\n Example\n\n<h1 \n style=\"font-size:60px;\">Heading 1</h1>\n\nTry it Yourself &raquo; (https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp?filename=tryhtml_headings_size)\n\n HTML Tag Reference\n\n W3Schools\' tag reference contains additional information about these tags and their attributes.\n\n Tag \n Description \n\n <html> (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/tag_html.asp) \n Defines the root of an HTML document \n\n <body> (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/tag_body.asp) \n Defines the document\'s body \n\n <h1> to <h6> (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/tag_hn.asp) \n Defines HTML headings \n\n For a complete list of all available HTML tags, visit our HTML Tag Reference (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/default.asp).\n\n Video: HTML Headings\n\n❮ Previous (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_challenges_attributes.asp)\nNext ❯ (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_exercise_embed.asp?topic=headings)\n\n ★\n\n +1 (https://profile.w3schools.com/log-in?redirect_url=https%3A%2F%2Fmy-learning.w3schools.com)\n\n Sign in to track progress (https://profile.w3schools.com/log-in?redirect_url=https%3A%2F%2Fpathfinder.w3schools.com)\n\n REMO