tps://www.w3schools.com/html/html_form_elements.asp)\n Exercises (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_exercise_embed.asp?topic=form_elements)\n Code Challenge (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_challenges_form_elements.asp)\n\nHTML Input Types (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_form_input_types.asp)\n \n Input Types (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_form_input_types.asp)\n Exercises (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_exercise_embed.asp?topic=form_input_types)\n Code Challenge (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_challenges_input_types.asp)\n\nHTML Input Attributes (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_form_attributes.asp)\n \n Input Attributes (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_form_attributes.asp)\n Exercises (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_exercise_embed.asp?topic=form_attributes)\n\nInput Form Attributes (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_form_attributes_form.asp)\n \n Input Form Attributes (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_form_attributes_form.asp)\n Exercises (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_exercise_embed.asp?topic=form_attributes_form)\n\n HTML Graphics\n\nHTML Canvas (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_canvas.asp)\n \n Canvas (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_canvas.asp)\n Code Challenge (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_challenges_canvas.asp)\n\nHTML SVG (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_svg.asp)\n \n SVG (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_svg.asp)\n Code Challenge (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_challenges_svg.asp)\n\n HTML Media\n\nHTML Media (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_media.asp)\nHTML Video (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_video.asp)\n \n Video (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_video.asp)\n Code Challenge (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_challenges_video.asp)\n\nHTML Audio (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_audio.asp)\n \n Audio (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_audio.asp)\n Code Challenge (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_challenges_audio.asp)\n\nHTML Plug-ins (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_object.asp)\nHTML YouTube (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_youtube.asp)\n\n Web APIs\n\nWeb APIs (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_api_whatis.asp)\nGeolocation API (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_geolocation.asp)\nDrag and Drop API (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_draganddrop.asp)\nPopover API (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_popover.asp)\nWeb Storage API (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_webstorage.asp)\nWeb Workers API (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_webworkers.asp)\nServer-Sent Events API (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_serversentevents.asp)\n\n HTML Cert\n\nHTML Certificate (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_exam.asp)\n\n HTML Examples\n\nHTML Examples (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_examples.asp)\nHTML Editor (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_editor.asp)\nHTML Quiz (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_quiz.asp)\nHTML Exercises (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_exercises.asp)\nHTML Challenges (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_challenges.asp)\nHTML Website (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_website.asp)\nHTML Syllabus (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_syllabus.asp)\nHTML Study Plan (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_study_plan.asp)\nHTML Interview Prep (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_interview_prep.asp)\nHTML Summary (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_summary.asp)\nHTML Accessibility (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_accessibility.asp)\n\n HTML References\n\nHTML Tag List (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/default.asp)\nHTML Attributes (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_attributes.asp)\nHTML Global Attributes (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_standardattributes.asp)\nHTML Browser Support (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_html_browsersupport.asp)\nHTML Events (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_eventattributes.asp)\nHTML Colors (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_colornames.asp)\nHTML Canvas (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_canvas.asp)\nHTML Audio/Video (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_av_dom.asp)\nHTML Doctypes (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_html_dtd.asp)\nHTML Character Sets (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_charactersets.asp)\nHTML URL Encode (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_urlencode.asp)\nHTML Lang Codes (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_language_codes.asp)\nHTTP Messages (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_httpmessages.asp)\nHTTP Methods (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_httpmethods.asp)\nPX to EM Converter (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_pxtoemconversion.asp)\nKeyboard Shortcuts (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/ref_keyboardshortcuts.asp)\n\n HTML Text Formatting \n\n❮ Previous (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_challenges_styles.asp)\nNext ❯ (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_exercise_embed.asp?topic=formatting)\n\n HTML contains several elements for defining text with a special meaning.\n\n Example\n\n This text is bold \n\n This text is italic \n\n This is subscript and superscript \n\nTry it Yourself &raquo; (https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp?filename=tryhtml_formatting_intro)\n\n Share \n\n Link Copied \n\n HTML Formatting Elements\n\n Formatting elements were designed to display special types of text:\n\n <b> - Bold text\n\n <strong> - Important text\n\n <i> - Italic text\n\n <em> - Emphasized text\n\n <mark> - Marked text\n\n <small> - Smaller text\n\n <del> - Deleted text\n\n <ins> - Inserted text\n\n <sub> - Subscript text\n\n <sup> - Superscript text\n\n HTML <b> and <strong> Elements\n\n The HTML <b> element defines bold text, \nwithout any extra importance.\n\n Example\n\n <b>This text is bold</b>\n\nTry it Yourself &raquo; (https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp?filename=tryhtml_formatting_b)\n\n The HTML <strong> element defines text \nwith strong importance. The content inside is typically displayed in bold.\n\n Example\n\n <strong>This text is \n important!</strong>\n\nTry it Yourself &raquo; (https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp?filename=tryhtml_formatting_strong)\n\n HTML <i> and <em> Elements\n\n The HTML <i> element defines a part of \ntext in an alternate voice or mood. The content inside is typically displayed in \nitalic.\n\n Tip: The <i> tag is often used to indicate a technical term, \na phrase from another language, a thought, a ship name, etc.\n\n Example\n\n <i>This text is italic</i>\n\nTry it Yourself &raquo; (https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp?filename=tryhtml_formatting_i)\n\n The HTML <em> element defines \nemphasized text. The content inside is typically displayed in italic.\n\n Tip: A screen reader will pronounce the words in <em> \nwith an emphasis, using verbal stress.\n\n Example\n\n <em>This text is \n emphasized</em>\n\nTry it Yourself &raquo; (https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp?filename=tryhtml_formatting_em)\n\n HTML <small> Element\n\n The HTML <small> element defines \nsmaller text:\n\n Example\n\n <small>This is some smaller text.</small>\n\nTry it Yourself &raquo; (https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp?filename=tryhtml_formatting_small)\n\n HTML <mark> Element\n\n The HTML <mark> element defines text \nthat should be marked or highlighted:\n\n Example\n\n <p>Do not forget to buy <mark>milk</mark> today.</p>\n\nTry it Yourself &raquo; (https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp?filename=tryhtml_formatting_mark)\n\n HTML <del> Element\n\n The HTML <del> element defines text \nthat has been deleted from a document. Browsers will usually strike a line \nthrough deleted text:\n\n Example\n\n <p>My favorite color is <del>blue</del> red.</p>\n\nTry it Yourself &raquo; (https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp?filename=tryhtml_formatting_del)\n\n HTML <ins> Element\n\n The HTML <ins> element defines a text \nthat has been inserted into a document. Browsers will usually underline inserted \ntext:\n\n Example\n\n <p>My favorite color is <del>blue</del> <ins>red</ins>.</p>\n\nTry it Yourself &raquo; (https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp?filename=tryhtml_formatting_del_ins)\n\n HTML <sub> Element\n\n The HTML <sub> element defines \nsubscript text. Subscript text appears half a character below the normal line, \nand is sometimes rendered in a smaller font. Subscript text can be used for \nchemical formulas, like H 2 O:\n\n Example\n\n <p>This \n is <sub>subscripted</sub> text.</p>\n\nTry it Yourself &raquo; (https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp?filename=tryhtml_formatting_sub)\n\n HTML <sup> Element\n\n The HTML <sup> element defines \nsuperscript text. Superscript text appears half a character above the normal \nline, and is sometimes rendered in a smaller font. Superscript text can be used \nfor footnotes, like WWW [1] :\n\n Example\n\n <p>This \n is <sup>superscripted</sup> text.</p>\n\nTry it Yourself &raquo; (https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp?filename=tryhtml_formatting_sup)\n\n HTML Text Formatting Elements\n\n Tag \n Description \n\n <b> (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/tag_b.asp) \n Defines bold text \n\n <em> (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/tag_em.asp) \n Defines emphasized text \n\n <i> (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/tag_i.asp) \n Defines a part of text in an alternate voice or mood \n\n <small> (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/tag_small.asp) \n Defines smaller text \n\n <strong> (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/tag_strong.asp) \n Defines important text \n\n <sub> (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/tag_sub.asp) \n Defines subscripted text \n\n <sup> (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/tag_sup.asp) \n Defines superscripted text \n\n <ins> (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/tag_ins.asp) \n Defines inserted text \n\n <del> (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/tag_del.asp) \n Defines deleted text \n\n <mark> (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/tag_mark.asp) \n Defines marked/highlighted text \n\n For a complete list of all available HTML tags, visit our HTML Tag Reference (https://www.w3schools.com/tags/default.asp).\n\n Video: HTML Formatting\n\n❮ Previous (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_challenges_styles.asp)\nNext ❯ (https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_