t are props?','SENT','{\"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:58:22'),(1389,92,7,36,'ASSISTANT','React components use props to communicate with each other. Every parent component can pass information to its child components by giving them props. Although props might remind you of HTML attributes, you can pass any JavaScript value through them, including objects, arrays, functions, and JSX. Additionally, parent components can pass shared state down to child components via props, a concept known as lifting state up.\n\nWould you like to read more about how to pass and read props in a component?','SENT','{\"answer\": \"React components use props to communicate with each other. Every parent component can pass information to its child components by giving them props. Although props might remind you of HTML attributes, you can pass any JavaScript value through them, including objects, arrays, functions, and JSX. Additionally, parent components can pass shared state down to child components via props, a concept known as lifting state up.\", \"suggestions\": [\"Passing Props\", \"Conditional Rendering\", \"Lifting State Up\"], \"follow_up_prompt\": \"Would you like to read more about how to pass and read props in a component?\", \"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:58:24'),(1390,92,7,36,'USER','How do components use props and state together?','SENT','{\"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:58:24'),(1391,92,7,36,'ASSISTANT','React components use props and state together to share data and keep components updated. When you want the state of multiple components to change together, you can remove the state from individual components, move it to their closest common parent, and then pass that state down to the child components via props. This practice is known as \"lifting state up.\" For example, a parent component can hold a state (such as an active index or a count) and pass it down to its children along with handler functions through props so the child components can display and respond to the shared state.\n\nWould you like to know more about sharing state between components or lifting state up?','SENT','{\"answer\": \"React components use props and state together to share data and keep components updated. When you want the state of multiple components to change together, you can remove the state from individual components, move it to their closest common parent, and then pass that state down to the child components via props. This practice is known as \\\"lifting state up.\\\" For example, a parent component can hold a state (such as an active index or a count) and pass it down to its children along with handler functions through props so the child components can display and respond to the shared state.\", \"suggestions\": [\"Sharing state between components\", \"Lifting state up\", \"Passing props to a component\"], \"follow_up_prompt\": \"Would you like to know more about sharing state between components or lifting state up?\", \"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:58:25'),(1392,92,7,36,'USER','What is useState and useEffect?','SENT','{\"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:58:25'),(1393,92,7,36,'ASSISTANT','useState is a React Hook that lets you add state to a component by declaring a state variable, taking an initial state, and returning a pair of values: the current state and a state setter function to update it. useEffect is another Hook that allows you to perform side effects or synchronize components with external systems (such as connecting to a chat server or manipulating a video player), and many Effects can also return a cleanup function to clean up after themselves.\n\nWould you like to know more about how the useState Hook updates state or how useEffect handles cleanups?','SENT','{\"answer\": \"useState is a React Hook that lets you add state to a component by declaring a state variable, taking an initial state, and returning a pair of values: the current state and a state setter function to update it. useEffect is another Hook that allows you to perform side effects or synchronize components with external systems (such as connecting to a chat server or manipulating a video player), and many Effects can also return a cleanup function to clean up after themselves.\", \"suggestions\": [\"useState Hook\", \"useEffect Hook\", \"Adding Interactivity\"], \"follow_up_prompt\": \"Would you like to know more about how the useState Hook updates state or how useEffect handles cleanups?\", \"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:58:27'),(1394,92,7,36,'USER','What is the difference between props and state?','SENT','{\"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:58:27'),(1395,92,7,36,'ASSISTANT','React components use props to communicate with each other, allowing a parent component to pass information down to its child components. Props can include any JavaScript value such as objects, arrays, functions, and JSX. On the other hand, state allows a component to hold and manage data that can change over time (such as a counter or active index). While props are passed down from a parent component, state can also be shared between components by moving it to their closest common parent and passing it down via props, a concept known as lifting state up.\n\nWould you like to learn more about how to pass props to a component or share state between components?','SENT','{\"answer\": \"React components use props to communicate with each other, allowing a parent component to pass information down to its child components. Props can include any JavaScript value such as objects, arrays, functions, and JSX. On the other hand, state allows a component to hold and manage data that can change over time (such as a counter or active index). While props are passed down from a parent component, state can also be shared between components by moving it to their closest common parent and passing it down via props, a concept known as lifting state up.\", \"suggestions\": [\"Passing props to a component\", \"Sharing state between components\", \"Lifting state up\"], \"follow_up_prompt\": \"Would you like to learn more about how to pass props to a component or share state between components?\", \"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:58:29'),(1396,92,7,36,'USER','How do I share state between two components?','SENT','{\"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:58:29'),(1397,92,7,36,'ASSISTANT','To share state between two components, you can use a practice called \"lifting state up\". By moving the state from the individual child components up to their closest common parent, you can hold the state there and then pass it down to the child components via props along with any necessary handler functions.\n\nWould you like to know more about how to structure state well or how to pass information without prop drilling?','SENT','{\"answer\": \"To share state between two components, you can use a practice called \\\"lifting state up\\\". By moving the state from the individual child components up to their closest common parent, you can hold the state there and then pass it down to the child components via props along with any necessary handler functions.\", \"suggestions\": [\"Lifting State Up\", \"Choosing State Structure\", \"Passing Data with Context\"], \"follow_up_prompt\": \"Would you like to know more about how to structure state well or how to pass information without prop drilling?\", \"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:58:30'),(1398,16,7,36,'USER','I said what is memosation','SENT','{\"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:58:31'),(1399,16,7,36,'ASSISTANT','I found information about how components \'remember\' information with state and handle user interactions, but I could not find sufficient information about memoization in the currently indexed website content.\n\nWould you like to learn more about how components remember information with state or handling user interactions?','SENT','{\"answer\": \"I found information about how components \'remember\' information with state and handle user interactions, but I could not find sufficient information about memoization in the currently indexed website content.\", \"suggestions\": [\"State and Memory\", \"Responding to Events\", \"Adding Interactivity\"], \"follow_up_prompt\": \"Would you like to learn more about how components remember information with state or handling user interactions?\", \"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:58:32'),(1400,16,7,36,'USER','okay tell me about responding to events','SENT','{\"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:58:51'),(1401,16,7,36,'USER','I want to tell me about responding to events','SENT','{\"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:59:06'),(1402,16,7,36,'ASSISTANT','React lets you add event handlers to your JSX to respond to user interactions like clicking, hovering, focusing on form inputs, and more. You can declare event handler functions inside your components and pass them down as props, such as using onClick={handleClick} without parentheses so React calls it only when the user interacts with the element.\n\nWould you like to learn more about adding event handlers or how components remember information with state?','SENT','{\"answer\": \"React lets you add event handlers to your JSX to respond to user interactions like clicking, hovering, focusing on form inputs, and more. You can declare event handler functions inside your components and pass them down as props, such as using onClick={handleClick} without parentheses so React calls it only when the user interacts with the element.\", \"suggestions\": [\"Adding Interactivity\", \"State A Components Memory\", \"Render and Commit\"], \"follow_up_prompt\": \"Would you like to learn more about adding event handlers or how components remember information with state?\", \"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:59:07'),(1403,16,7,36,'USER','can you tell me the difference between tops and state','SENT','{\"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:59:32'),(1404,16,7,36,'ASSISTANT','I found information about state, which acts as a component\'s memory to remember values and update them on interaction, but I could not find information about \'tops\' (or props) in the currently indexed website content.\n\nWould you like to learn more about a component\'s memory with state or managing state?','SENT','{\"answer\": \"I found information about state, which acts as a component\'s memory to remember values and update them on interaction, but I could not find information about \'tops\' (or props) in the currently indexed website content.\", \"suggestions\": [\"What is Django?\", \"How do I get started with Django?\", \"Where can I find the documentation?\"], \"follow_up_prompt\": \"Would you like to learn more about a component\'s memory with state or managing state?\", \"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:59:34'),(1405,16,7,36,'USER','no no I asked can you tell me about props and state','SENT','{\"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:59:45'),(1406,16,7,36,'ASSISTANT','Props and state are both types of information used in React components, but they serve different purposes:\n\n* **Props**: React components use props to communicate with each other. Every parent component can pass information to its child components by giving them props (similar to HTML attributes), and you can pass any JavaScript value through them including objects, arrays, functions, and JSX.\n* **State**: State acts as a component\'s memory. It allows a component to remember values (like an index or a toggle status) and update them in response to user interactions.\n\nWould you like to learn more about passing props to a component or state as a component\'s memory?','SENT','{\"answer\": \"Props and state are both types of information used in React components, but they serve different purposes:\\n\\n* **Props**: React components use props to communicate with each other. Every parent component can pass information to its child components by giving them props (similar to HTML attributes), and you can pass any JavaScript value through them including objects, arrays, functions, and JSX.\\n* **State**: State acts as a component\'s memory. It allows a component to remember values (like an index or a toggle status) and update them in response to user interactions.\", \"suggestions\": [\"Passing props to a component\", \"State a component\'s memory\", \"Managing state\"], \"follow_up_prompt\": \"Would you like to learn more about passing props to a component or state as a component\'s memory?\", \"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e755dc766aa1561e2feb\"}','2026-10-05 06:59:48'),(1407,16,7,36,'USER','now tell me who is Jitendra Kumar','SENT','{\"source_fingerprint\": \"3dc86e6666d7a5f236ea594bf10bb95726c2fa7538d7e